00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.850
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: With over
300 sessions at NEXT 2018,

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.680
it's understandable you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.660
So if you didn't see "What's
New with TensorFlow,"

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.451
then stay tuned, because
here's the rewind.

00:00:08.451 --> 00:00:11.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.311
So what's TensorFlow?

00:00:16.311 --> 00:00:18.060
TensorFlow is a machine
learning framework

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.976
that's been used to go
hunting for new planets

00:00:19.976 --> 00:00:22.050
and discover solar systems
similar to our own,

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.210
prevent blindness by
helping doctors catch

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.790
diabetic retinopathy
early, and save rainforests

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:30.159
by alerting authorities to signs
of illegal logging activity.

00:00:30.159 --> 00:00:31.950
It's what AlphaGo and
Google's Cloud Vision

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.290
are built on top of, and
it's yours to play with.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:35.650
TensorFlow is open source.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.979
You can download it for
free and get started today.

00:00:37.979 --> 00:00:40.020
And we've been working to
make it easier and more

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.210
useful than ever before.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.740
Here are some of my
favorite highlights.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.675
Let's start with
TensorFlow Eager.

00:00:46.675 --> 00:00:48.300
If you tried TensorFlow
in the old days

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.966
and ran away screaming
because it forced

00:00:49.966 --> 00:00:52.290
you to code like an
academic or alien

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.774
instead of like a developer,
there's great news for you.

00:00:54.774 --> 00:00:56.940
TensorFlow Eager Execution
lets you interact with it

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.920
like a pure Python programmer.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.400
All the immediacy of writing
and debugging line by line,

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.739
instead of holding
your breath while you

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:06.420
build those huge graphs.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.540
Keras plus TensorFLow
equals easier neural network

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:10.290
construction.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.060
If you like
object-oriented thinking

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.860
and you like building neural
networks one layer at a time,

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:16.350
I think you'll love tf.keras.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.660
In just these few
lines of code, we've

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.357
created a sequential
neural network

00:01:20.357 --> 00:01:21.690
with all the bells and whistles.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.010
New languages.

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:24.160
You asked, and we listened.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.370
TensorFlow is not just
for Pythonistas anymore.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:29.700
It now runs in many languages,
from R to Swift to JavaScript.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.000
And speaking of JavaScript, you
can train and execute models

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.430
right in the browser
with TensorFlow.js.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.530
TensorFlow Lite
brings model execution

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.470
to a variety of devices,
including mobile and IoT,

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.440
giving you more than a 3x
boost in inference speed-up.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.930
Yes, now you can get machine
learning on your Raspberry

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.130
Pi or your phone.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.770
See the full talk for a live
demo of image classification

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.390
on an Android emulator.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:54.536
Satisfy your need for
speed with Cloud TPUs.

00:01:54.536 --> 00:01:56.160
If you're tired of
waiting for your CPU

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.250
to finish churning through
your data to train your model,

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.950
you can now get your hands on
hardware specially designed

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:02.830
for the job.

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:06.000
And we've just announced
the V3 TPUs in alpha.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.729
Next, make your input
processing more expressive

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:11.240
and efficient with
the TF.data namespace.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:13.790
TF.data gives you fast,
flexible, easy-to-use data

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:16.200
pipelines synchronized
with training.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.150
And finally, you can
find and share solutions

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.160
on TensorFlow Hub,
which is a repository

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.350
for reusable, pre-trained
machine learning model

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.605
components, packaged
for one-line reuse,

00:02:25.605 --> 00:02:26.444
which is awesome.

00:02:26.444 --> 00:02:28.110
So that's it for my
favorite highlights.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.200
If you'd like to
see the full talk,

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.210
check out the link in
the description below.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:33.750
And don't forget to subscribe
to the Cloud YouTube channel

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:37.790
for more great NEXT rewind on
content thanks for watching.

